// Spoofed transfer event: a log carrying the standard ERC-20/ERC-721
// `Transfer(address,address,uint256)` event's topic0
// (`0xddf252ad1be2c89b69c2b068fc378daa952ba7f163c4a11628f55a4df523b3ef`)
// that is not actually shaped like a decodable transfer.
//
// Why this pair of facts is the right structural signature: an
// off-chain observer (an exchange's deposit listener, a wallet, a
// naive indexer) that recognizes a "token deposit" by filtering logs
// on topic0 alone — a common shortcut, since topic0 is cheap to
// filter on at the RPC layer (`eth_getLogs`'s own `topics[0]` filter)
// — can be fooled by any contract that emits a log with that exact
// topic0 but without the two indexed addresses and 32-byte amount a
// real `Transfer` carries. This is the real, publicly documented "fake
// deposit event" technique used against exchanges that trust logs
// without validating their full shape. `ingestion`'s own
// `try_decode_token_transfer` (`crates/ingestion/src/normalize.rs`)
// already refuses to produce a `fact_model::TokenTransfer` for a log
// that doesn't have exactly 3 topics and 32 bytes of data — this
// pattern's only new capability is letting a DSL clause ask about that
// refusal directly, via the new `log(topic0: ..., decoded: ...)`
// predicate (Milestone 12; see `crates/dsl/src/schema.rs`).
//
// Both `topic0` and `decoded` are fully, structurally checkable from
// facts `fact-model` already records (`LogEvent.topics`, and whether
// any `TokenTransfer.log_id` cites this log) — neither is an invented
// or interpreted fact the way `storage(role: ...)` or
// `token_transfer(unexpected: ...)` are. `topic0` is a raw byte
// comparison against a publicly computable `keccak256` hash, exactly
// the same idiom `call(selector: ...)` already established (Milestone
// 7) for function selectors, just applied to the analogous event-side
// concept.
//
// Scope note: this pattern flags "a log claims to be a Transfer event
// but isn't shaped like one" as a reviewable candidate. It cannot (and
// does not claim to) determine whether the log's emitter intended to
// deceive an off-chain observer, whether any observer was actually
// fooled, or whether the log's true purpose was legitimate (e.g. a
// custom, non-standard event that happens to share this exact topic0
// hash by construction is vanishingly unlikely but not structurally
// impossible to rule out) — the fact model has no "author's intent"
// fact, only the log's own recorded shape.
pattern spoofed_transfer_event version 1 {
    family: SpoofedTransferEvent
    severity: High
    tags: ["event-spoofing", "fake-deposit", "log-injection"]
    references: ["SCWE-063"]

    evidence {
        required fake_transfer: log(
            topic0: "0xddf252ad1be2c89b69c2b068fc378daa952ba7f163c4a11628f55a4df523b3ef",
            decoded: false
        )
    }

    constraint: fake_transfer
}
